.Dd June 4, 2026
.Dt XTC_DUMP 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm xtc_dump ,
.Nm xtc_panic ,
.Nm xtc_crash_handler_install
.Nd crash diagnostics: runtime-state dump, panic, fatal-signal handler
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In xtc.h
.In xtc_dump.h
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_dump "int fd"
.Ft void
.Fn xtc_panic "const char *file" "int line" "const char *fmt" "..."
.Ft int
.Fn xtc_crash_handler_install "void"
.Fn XTC_PANIC "fmt" "..."
.Fn XTC_ASSERT "cond"
.Fn XTC_ASSERT_F "cond" "fmt" "..."
.Sh DESCRIPTION
These routines turn the live runtime into a human-readable diagnostic
on the way down, without a debugger or a core file.  The dump is the
in-process counterpart of the debugger scripts
.Pq Xr xtc_proc 3 , Xr xtc_inspect 3 :
the same proc, loop, and mailbox view an
.Sy observer
session shows, written to a file descriptor.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_dump
writes a best-effort runtime-state dump to
.Fa fd
.Pq commonly Dv STDERR_FILENO :
.Bl -enum -offset indent -compact
.It
the C backtrace of the
.Em calling
OS thread, when a backtrace backend is compiled in
.Pq see Sx PORTABILITY ;
.It
every scheduler loop with its run-queue and work-steal counters;
.It
every live proc with its run state, park reason, and mailbox depth,
peak, capacity, and lifetime receive/drop counts.
.El
The field labels match the debugger scripts, so an in-process dump and
a
.Xr gdb 1
session read alike.
.Fn xtc_dump
never allocates and never aborts; it writes through a fixed stack
buffer with raw
.Xr write 2
calls, so it can run at any time and does not perturb the heap it may
be diagnosing.  The caller decides what to do next.
.Pp
.Fn xtc_panic
emits
.Fa fmt Ns / Ns Fa ...
as a panic banner, then
.Fn xtc_dump STDERR_FILENO ,
then calls
.Xr abort 3 .
It does not return.  Call it through the
.Fn XTC_PANIC
macro, which supplies
.Fa file
and
.Fa line :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
XTC_PANIC("bad frame %ld: pin=%d", (long)idx, pin);
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn XTC_ASSERT
panics with the failed expression when
.Fa cond
is false;
.Fn XTC_ASSERT_F
does the same with a caller-supplied message.  Unlike the standard
.Xr assert 3 ,
both emit the full runtime dump before aborting, and both are always
compiled in
.Pq they are diagnostics, not debug-only checks .
.Bd -literal -offset indent
XTC_ASSERT(frame->pin > 0);
XTC_ASSERT_F(n <= cap, "overflow: n=%zu cap=%zu", n, cap);
.Ed
.Pp
.Fn xtc_crash_handler_install
installs a handler for the fatal signals
.Dv SIGSEGV , SIGBUS , SIGABRT , SIGFPE ,
and
.Dv SIGILL
that emits a backtrace and a best-effort runtime dump to standard
error, then re-raises the signal with its default disposition so a
core is still produced.  It is idempotent and installs handlers only
for faults -- never for
.Dv SIGCHLD , SIGTERM ,
or other signals an embedder may manage.  It returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
on a platform without POSIX signals.
.Sh SCOPE AND LIMITS
A
.Em parked
fiber's own C stack is not unwound: it lives in a saved coroutine
context, not on a live OS thread.  Like an Erlang crash dump or a Go
panic, the dump reports each proc's state and mailbox -- which proc is
parked, on what, with how deep a mailbox -- not N reconstructed C
stacks.  Only the calling (faulting) thread's C stack is shown.  For
deep per-fiber stacks attach a debugger to a core; the dump and the
debugger share field names so the two views line up.
.Pp
This makes the facility strongest for the failure mode it was built
for: a hung or wedged message-passing system, where the question is
.Dq which proc is waiting on whom, and why did it not wake.
For domain data-structure invariants, keep using
.Fn XTC_ASSERT
on those structures directly.
.Sh SIGNAL SAFETY
.Fn xtc_dump
takes the per-loop inspection locks
.Pq via Xr xtc_inspect 3 ,
so it is fully reliable from an explicit
.Fn XTC_PANIC
or
.Fn XTC_ASSERT
in normal context.  From the fatal-signal handler the banner and
backtrace are always emitted -- they use only async-signal-safe
primitives -- but the proc/loop walk is attempted best-effort and may
block if the fault happened while a loop lock was held.  A hung
handler still leaves the banner and backtrace, and the re-raised
signal still yields a core.
.Sh PORTABILITY
The C backtrace uses the
.Fn __os_backtrace
seam, selected at configure time: the
.In execinfo.h
.Fn backtrace
backend on glibc, macOS, and the BSDs;
otherwise a no-op stub.  Where no backend is present the dump omits the
C stack but still reports the full proc/loop/mailbox state.
.Fn xtc_crash_handler_install
returns
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
on Windows
.Pq SEH is future work .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn xtc_dump
returns nothing.
.Fn xtc_panic
does not return.
.Fn xtc_crash_handler_install
returns
.Dv XTC_OK ,
or
.Dv XTC_E_NOSYS
where unsupported.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Install the crash handler at startup, then assert an invariant:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
xtc_crash_handler_install();
\&...
XTC_ASSERT_F(pin > 0, "unfix underflow: frame=%ld", (long)idx);
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr xtc_inspect 3 ,
.Xr xtc_proc 3 ,
.Xr xtc_loop 3 ,
.Xr xtc_slab 3
.Sh AUTHORS
